    <help><![CDATA[
**What it does**

This tool turns eDNA metabarcoding results into the Darwin Core tables that OBIS and GBIF publish,
using `edna2obis <https://github.com/aomlomics/edna2obis>`_. It writes:

- an **occurrence** table, with one row for each ASV in each library that has reads, leaving out
  the control samples;
- the **DNA-derived data** extension, which gives the primers, target gene, sequence and methods for
  each occurrence;
- if you ask for them, an **extended Measurement Or Fact (eMoF)** extension and an **Event core**;
- the **eml.xml** dataset metadata and **meta.xml**, zipped with the tables as a Darwin Core archive
  that you can load into an IPT (Integrated Publishing Toolkit) for OBIS or GBIF.

-----

**Inputs**

- A **FAIRe workbook** (.xlsx) with projectMetadata, sampleMetadata and experimentRunMetadata
  sheets.
- For each assay, and each analysis of it, a **taxonomy table** and an **abundance table**, as
  Tourmaline writes them or as in the FAIRe taxaRaw and otuRaw templates.
- The **taxonomy**. You can give a reviewed mapping table from **edna2obis: match taxa**, which we
  recommend, because you can check the matches first and the tool then needs no internet access.
  Or the tool can look the names up in WoRMS or GBIF during the run.

**Outputs**

- **Darwin Core archive** (.zip): meta.xml, the tables it lists, and eml.xml.
- The tables on their own (CSV): **occurrence**, **DNA-derived data**, **eMoF** and **event core**.
- **eml.xml**.
- **taxa mapping**: the candidate matches for each name and the one selected, as edna2obis writes
  it.
- **report**: edna2obis's HTML report, with previews of each step and all the warnings. Galaxy may
  remove the report's sample map, because it loads a script from the internet.
- **config**: the edna2obis config.yaml that was used, so you can repeat the run outside Galaxy.
- **log**: what the tool and edna2obis did, with all the warnings.

-----

**What the tool adds to edna2obis**

- Workbooks from FAIRe-ator work as they are, including single-assay workbooks with the assay
  details in project_level. The tool first relabels the assay columns by their assay_name.
- **eventDate is never misread.** Excel date cells and year-first text (2019-06-07) pass through as
  ISO 8601. On its own, edna2obis reads 7/6/19 as 6 July but 13/6/19 as 13 June, so a day-first
  column comes out partly swapped. The tool stops on such dates and lists them, unless you say on
  the form whether they are written day first (as in Australia) or month first (as in the USA).
  FAIRe missing-value terms such as *not applicable* are kept.
- **Text such as NA or N/A** is replaced with one of FAIRe's missing-value terms, by default
  *missing: not provided*, and the log lists the fields changed. You can keep it as written, as
  edna2obis does, or stop and list the cells.
- **Values reach the tables as written.** On its own, edna2obis reads a column of IDs such as 001,
  002 as numbers (1, 2), and its final check of the output tables empties text such as NA. The tool
  replaces these two reads while edna2obis runs, and stops if edna2obis no longer reads that way.
- Abundance tables may have the two FAIRe header rows above the field names, and columns named by
  samp_name. The tool renames these columns to the lib_id of the sample's run, so that control
  samples are left out as they should be.
- eventDate Excel date cells are written as ISO 8601 text.
- When edna2obis reports an error (for example in the eMoF or EML step), when a name is missing
  from the mapping table, or when a request to WoRMS or GBIF fails, the tool stops with a message,
  so it never writes incomplete files.

@HELP_MAPPING_COLUMNS@

@HELP_MATCHING@

-----

**occurrenceID**

edna2obis builds each occurrenceID as ``project_id:analysis run name:lib_id_occ_featureid``. Keep
the analysis run names the same when you publish a new version of the dataset, so that the IDs stay
stable.

**eMoF template**

edna2obis's template lists FAIRe fields such as temp, salinity, diss_oxygen and input_read_count,
with NERC vocabulary terms and units. Fields missing from the workbook are skipped. A unit of
``provided`` takes the unit from the matching ``_unit`` field (for example diss_oxygen_unit). The
template's first row (expedition_id = GU19-01) is specific to NOAA's example and matches nothing in
other datasets. The eMoF step joins runs by lib_id only, so it fails when one lib_id is used in two
assays. In that case, use a template without experimentRunMetadata fields.

